---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
---
<Base title="Bio" description="Biography of Richard W. Evans, PhD — macroeconomist and open-source policy modeler.">
  <header class="page-header">
    <div class="wrap wrap-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Bio</h1>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="wrap wrap-narrow prose section">
    <p>
      <strong>Richard W. Evans</strong> is a macroeconomist whose work sits at the intersection of
      public economics, computational methods, and open-source software. He is a Senior Economist at
      the <a href="https://abundance.institute" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Abundance Institute</a>
      in Salt Lake City, Director and Founder of the Open Source Economics Laboratory (OSE Lab), and
      President and Co-Founder of Open Research Group, Inc. (OpenRG).
    </p>
    <p>
      His research develops large-scale overlapping generations (OLG) macroeconomic models for the
      dynamic analysis of fiscal policy. He is a core maintainer of the open-source
      <a href="https://github.com/PSLmodels/OG-Core" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OG-Core</a>
      model and its country calibrations — including
      <a href="https://github.com/PSLmodels/OG-USA" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OG-USA</a> — which
      are used by researchers and policy institutions to quantify the macroeconomic and distributional
      effects of tax and budget reforms.
    </p>
    <p>
      Evans previously served as Senior Research Fellow and Director of Open Policy at the Center for
      Growth and Opportunity at Utah State University, Associate Director of the Master of Arts Program
      in Computational Social Science at the University of Chicago, Economist at the Open Source Policy
      Center of the American Enterprise Institute, and Assistant Professor of Economics at Brigham Young
      University, where he co-founded the BYU Macroeconomics and Computational Laboratory. He is a strong
      advocate for open-source methods, reproducibility, and transparency in economic policy analysis.
    </p>
    <p>
      He earned his Ph.D. in Economics from the University of Texas at Austin in 2008. He is a co-author
      of the forthcoming books <em>Redesigning Aging: The Ethics and Economics of Human Longevity</em>
      (Harvard University Press) and <em>Overlapping Generations Models for Policy Analysis: Theory and
      Computation</em>.
    </p>

    <h2>Fields of specialization</h2>
    <ul>
      {site.fields.map((f) => <li>{f}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <h2>Education</h2>
    <ul>
      {site.education.map((e) => <li>{e.degree} &mdash; {e.school}, {e.year}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <p style="margin-top:2rem;">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href={site.cvPath} target="_blank" rel="noopener">View full CV (PDF)</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
